Missing: typeof() is int to the parser, so its argument and the declared type are lost

src/startup.c predefines "typeof(xxx) int" and "__typeof__(xxx) int". So a name inside
typeof() is not a reference, and a variable declared with typeof() has the type int. A
field reached through it, as in typeof(*p) *q = p; q->y, gets no reference. In GNU and
kernel-style code typeof() is mostly inside macros: container_of(), min() and max(),
cleanup macros. Since C23 typeof is standard.

The likely fix:
- A keyword token TYPEOF in src/init.c, beside sizeof, for typeof, __typeof__,
  __typeof and maybe typeof_unqual. Remove the two fallback macros.
- Two alternatives in type_specifier2 in src/c_parser.y:
    TYPEOF '(' expr ')'       -> the expression's typeModifier
    TYPEOF '(' type_name ')'  -> the type name's modifier
  The parser already tells ( expr ) from ( type_name ) for sizeof, through the
  lexer's TYPE_NAME.

To check first:
- A declarator such as typeof(p) *q builds on the base type. If it changes the chain
  it gets, p's type changes too, and the modifier must be copied. The struct
  specifiers already hand out shared modifiers, so it is probably safe.
- src/yacc_parser.y has a copy of the C grammar and may need the same rules.
- byacc may report new conflicts.
- typeof becomes a keyword. The fallback macro already reserves it as a
  function-like macro, so little changes.

Found by reading, after offsetof() had the same kind of gap (Mac, session f5b5c93a,
2026-11-24).
